   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the correction law, in relation to local correctional facility inmates held in secure facilities operated by the office of mental health",
    "basePrintNo": "S5430",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5430-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §508, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5430\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the correction law, in relation to\nlocal correctional facility inmates held in secure facilities operated\nby the office of mental health\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nTo amend the Correction Law to authorize the Office of Mental Health\n(OMH) to enter into an agreement with a sheriff's department to permit\nthe relevant public safety officials to transfer custody of an inmate\nto an OMH secure facility while such inmate receives mental health\ntreatment.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Correction L. § 508(2)(1)(a) to\nclarify that inmates who are in a secure OMH facility are not in the\ncustody of a sheriff.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would authorize the public safety officials of a\nlocal correctional facility, pursuant to an agreement with OMH, to\ntransfer custody of an inmate to OMH when such inmate needs to receive\neither a medical diagnosis or treatment in a secure facility operated\nby OMH.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would define what constitutes an OMH secure\nfacility authorized to receive and retain certain inmates.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would make the bill effective on the 120th day\nafter enactment.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCorrection L. § 508 authorizes inmates to be transferred from a jail\nto a hospital when they necessitate physical or mental health care.\nWhile the inmate is in the hospital, the inmate remains under the\nsupervision of the public safety officials of the jail from which he\nor she came. However, the public safety officials do not serve a\nmeaningful function when stationed at OMH secure facilities because\nthose facilities are already staffed with personnel who are trained in\nforensic security.\n\nTo address this concern, and to use law enforcement resources more\nefficiently, this bill would allow the relevant public safety\nofficials to transfer the inmate to the custody of an OMH secure\nfacility when an inmate is in need of mental healthcare. Such\ncustodial transfer would be executed pursuant to an agreement between\nthe relevant public safety officials and OMH.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect 120 days after it becomes a law.\n",
